.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt BIND 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bind
.Nd assign a socket's local address, port or interface
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn bind "int fd" "const struct sockaddr *addr" "socklen_t addrlen"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn bind
sets the local address and port, or the interface, of the socket
.Fa fd
from the address at
.Fa addr .
.Fa addrlen
is the address length in bytes.
The kernel interface is
.Xr socketcall 2
operation 2.
.Ss IPv4 sockets
For TCP and UDP, the address and port are read in network byte order from a
.Vt sockaddr_in .
At least eight address bytes are required.
The family field is not checked for these socket types.
The address must be zero, for any of the machine's addresses, one in
.Ql 127/8 ,
one an interface has, or for UDP a broadcast address; another gives
.Er EADDRNOTAVAIL .
A socket bound to an address receives only what arrives for that address,
so a server bound to 127.0.0.1 is reached from this machine alone, as on
Linux.
A nonzero port below 1024 requires effective user ID 0.
.Pp
A UDP bind registers the address and port for receiving datagrams.
Port zero selects a free port from 49152 through 65535.
Two bindings of a port meet when one is to any address or both are to the
same one; a socket may take a port at an address that meets another's only
when all sockets so met have
.Dv SO_REUSEADDR
set and the binding owners have the same effective user ID.
Rebinding first removes the socket's previous UDP registration, even
when the new registration fails.
.Pp
A TCP bind records the address and port.
It rejects a nonzero port that already has a live listener at an address
that meets the one asked for, but does not reserve the port against
another bind before
.Xr listen 2 .
Binding TCP port zero selects a free port from 49152 through 65535 that
no TCP connection or listener uses and no other stream socket has bound, as
Linux does, and
.Xr getsockname 2
reports it.
.Pp
There is no loopback interface, but
.Ql 127/8
is this machine's, as on Linux: what is sent to it goes round the
kernel's loopback ring from 127.0.0.1, and what arrives from the wire for
it is dropped.
.Pp
A raw ICMP socket accepts address zero, an interface's address, or
an address in
.Ql 127/8 .
Another address gives
.Er EADDRNOTAVAIL .
The raw socket used only for interface queries rejects binding.
.Ss Packet sockets
An
.Dv AF_PACKET
socket takes a 20-byte
.Vt sockaddr_ll .
Its interface index may be zero for all interfaces or the index of an
existing interface.
The protocol, in network byte order, may select IPv4 or ARP; zero keeps
the socket's existing protocol.
Changing the interface or protocol discards queued packets.
.Ss Netlink sockets
An
.Dv AF_NETLINK
socket takes a 12-byte
.Vt sockaddr_nl :
its port id, which 0 has the kernel pick, and its multicast groups, which
are kept and never sent to, as
.Xr netlink 7
describes.
A port id another netlink socket has gives
.Er EADDRINUSE .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn bind
returns 0 on success.
On failure it returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The raw kernel call returns a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default-suite probe
.Pa tests/loopprobe.c
binds a UDP socket to address zero and port 7100, then sends a datagram
to
.Ql 127.0.0.1:7100
and reads it back.
It also checks that two TCP binds can precede a listener, but a bind
to an existing listener's port fails with
.Er EADDRINUSE ,
that a bind to an address the machine lacks fails with
.Er EADDRNOTAVAIL ,
and that a listener bound to 127.0.0.1 refuses a connection to the
machine's interface address.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EADDRNOTAVAIL
.It Er EACCES
A caller without effective user ID 0 requested a nonzero port below 1024.
.It Er EADDRINUSE
A UDP port cannot be shared, no ephemeral port is available, or memory
for a UDP binding cannot be allocated.
A TCP port already has a live listener at an address that meets the one
asked for, or no ephemeral port is free for a bind to port zero.
.It Er EADDRNOTAVAIL
The address is not zero, not in
.Ql 127/8 ,
not an interface's, and for UDP not a broadcast address.
.It Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Er EFAULT
The socketcall argument array or the required address bytes cannot be read.
.It Er EINVAL
The address length is negative or less than eight bytes, a packet
address is shorter than 20 bytes, or a packet protocol is neither zero,
IPv4 nor ARP.
.It Er ENOBUFS
Memory for a packet-socket binding cannot be allocated.
.It Er ENODEV
The packet address names an interface index other than zero or an
existing interface.
.It Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa fd
does not refer to a socket.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
The socket is the raw descriptor used only for interface queries.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr accept 2 ,
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr getsockname 2 ,
.Xr listen 2 ,
.Xr setsockopt 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2
